course: politics
lesson: 2
title: Elections and voting: ballots, counting and winning
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: An election is how a whole country, town or school chooses who will be in charge: everyone allowed to vote gets one say, and the votes are counted to find the winner.
explain: On election day an ordinary place, maybe the hall where you do PE, becomes a polling station: a voter's name is checked on a list, they mark a paper in a private booth, fold it and drop it into a sealed box.
explain: Your vote is secret, so nobody can bully or pay you to vote their way; Mum can tell you who she chose if she wants to, but she never has to.
explain: After the polls close, the papers are counted, often by hand in big halls late at night, with watchers from every party making sure it is fair; a close result can be counted again, and the loser usually congratulates the winner.
explain: TV may show guesses early, like an exit poll at 10 pm or a map turning red and blue, but a guess is not a result: the real answer only comes when every vote is counted.
fact: Voting is secret: a secret ballot means nobody sees your choice, so nobody can bully or pay you to vote their way.
fact: A polling station is the place, often a school or church hall, where people go to vote; they mark a ballot paper in a private booth and put it into a sealed ballot box.
fact: In the UK and Ireland paper votes are counted by hand in front of observers from every party; in much of the USA, machines scan paper ballots, and the paper can be checked again.
fact: Many countries let people vote by post or early, so people who are ill, away or busy can still vote.
fact: A recount is counting the votes again because the result is very close; it is a normal check, not cheating, and the result rarely changes.
fact: To concede is to accept that you lost; it shows supporters the election was fair and lets power pass peacefully to the winner.
fact: In the US presidential election, each state gives its votes to the winner in that state (two small states split theirs); this is the Electoral College, and TV shows it as a red (Republican) and blue (Democrat) map.
fact: A candidate needs 270 of the 538 Electoral College votes to become US president.
fact: A campaign is everything a candidate does to win votes, like posters, speeches and knocking on doors (canvassing); damaging someone's poster is wrong even if you disagree.
fact: A landslide is winning by a huge amount; a narrow win is a very close one.
fact: An exit poll asks some voters how they voted as they leave, then guesses the result; it is often close to the real result, but not always right.
fact: People disagree about voting by post: some say it lets more people take part, others say it is harder to check who filled in the form.
fact: People disagree about the Electoral College: some say it makes candidates care about smaller states, others say the winner should simply be whoever gets the most votes in the whole country.
exercise: Liam asks Mum who she voted for, and she says "That's a secret." Why is voting secret? | So nobody can bully or pay you to vote their way | So the counters do not know how many people voted | Because it is against the law to tell anyone ever | So that candidates do not know they are standing
exercise: Why is Zara's school closed for the day? | Its hall is being used as a polling station | All the teachers are standing as candidates | Children must count the votes in class | The government closes schools to stop protests
exercise: What happens inside a polling station? | Names are checked, people mark a paper in a booth and drop it in a sealed box | People shout their choice out loud to a counter | Everyone puts their hand up and a teacher counts | Voters phone the candidate they like best
exercise: Why are UK votes counted by hand in front of watchers? | Anyone can see it is fair, even if it is slow | Computers have not been invented in the UK | Watchers are there to change votes they dislike | Counting by hand is faster than any machine
exercise: How did Hannah's gran vote weeks early? | She filled in a postal vote and sent it by post | She phoned the prime minister and told him her choice | She voted in a dream and it was counted | She asked her neighbour to vote twice for her
exercise: A candidate loses by twelve votes and asks for a recount. Is that cheating? | No, a recount is a normal check when it is close | Yes, asking for a recount is always against the law | Yes, because only the winner may ask for one | No, because the loser gets to choose the result
exercise: Why does the losing candidate say "I congratulate my opponent"? | Conceding shows the vote was fair and power can change peacefully | She is secretly happy that she lost the race | The law makes her give the winner a present | She hopes the winner will hand the job back
exercise: Grace sees a US map turning red and blue. What does it show? | Which party won each state in the race for president | Which states are hot and which are cold on election night | Where the two parties have their offices | Which states have the most people asleep
exercise: How many Electoral College votes does a US candidate need to win? | 270 out of 538 | 50 out of 100 | 100 out of 200 | 435 out of 538
exercise: Someone tears down a candidate's poster from outside a house. What is the right view? | It is wrong, even if you disagree with the candidate | It is fine, if you dislike that candidate | It is fine, because posters on the street belong to nobody | It is wrong, but only during the summer
exercise: The radio says someone won "by a landslide". What does that mean? | They won by a huge amount | A mountain fell on the losing candidate | They won by only a few votes | The election had to be held outdoors
exercise: At 10 pm the TV shows an exit poll before any votes are counted. How do they "know already"? | They asked some voters on the way out and made a guess | They secretly opened all the ballot boxes early in the morning | The winner is always decided before polling day | They counted every vote in one second with a robot
exercise: What is an exit poll? | A guess built from asking voters leaving the polling station | The final official result after every vote is counted | A wooden pole outside the polling station door | A vote held only for people leaving the country
exercise: What do people disagree about with postal voting? | It lets more people vote, but it is harder to check | Whether stamps should show the candidate's face on them | Whether post offices are allowed to exist | Whether letters can carry paper at all
exercise: What is canvassing? | Going door to door to ask people for their vote | Painting a big picture of a candidate on a canvas | Counting all of the votes inside a big white tent | Tearing down the posters put up by a rival candidate
test: Your mate goes, "u can just tell the counter who u voted for innit, it's no big deal." What should you say? | The vote is secret, so nobody can push you, but you can share it if you want | He's right, everyone must say their vote out loud | He's wrong, telling anyone is a crime forever | The counter already knows, because he reads your mind
test: Grandad, a Geordie, says, "Howay man, we're gannin' to the count, it's canny exciting!" ("Howay" means come on, "gannin'" means going, "canny" means really.) What will he see? | People counting paper votes by hand while party watchers check | A computer printing the winner's name on a screen in a single second | The candidates counting their own votes in secret | The king choosing the winner from a hat
test: Finn asks, "Why don't they just use a computer?" Which is the best answer? | Hand counting is slow, but anyone can watch it and trust it | Computers are banned from every building in the UK | Hand counting is actually faster than any computer could ever be | Computers are only used to count votes for kids
test: In the US, cousin Jake texts, "y'all, our state flipped red, it's lowkey wild." ("Flipped red" means the Republican won the state.) What does this mean for the race? | The Republican candidate won that state's Electoral College votes | Everyone in the state changed their T-shirts to red | The state has left the United States | The state's votes are thrown away completely
test: Your sister says, "The loser asked for a recount, so they must be a cheat, fr." ("fr" means "for real".) What's wrong with that? | A recount is a normal fair check when a result is very close | Nothing, asking for a recount proves cheating | Recounts are only for winners who want even more votes than they got | Recounts are illegal in every country
test: What would happen if losing candidates never accepted fair results? | Handing power to the winner could become a fight instead of peaceful | Nothing at all would change after elections | The losing candidate would win automatically | Elections would get faster and calmer
test: Grandad grumbles, "Exit poll says Party A, so it's done, turn the telly off, it's a right bummer." Is it done? | No, it's a guess; only the full count gives the result | Yes, the exit poll is the official result, announced on telly | Yes, because exit polls are never wrong about the winner | No, because exit polls are only about the weather that day
test: Your Aussie mate says, "Me nan voted early at the arvo booth, then had a sausage." ("Arvo" means afternoon.) What kind of voting did nan do? | Early voting, before the main election day | Postal voting, with a sausage in the envelope | No voting, because early votes are thrown out | Double voting, once early and once on the day
test: A kid on TikTok says, "landslide = the winner got buried in mud." What does it really mean? | Winning by a very big amount | Losing by a very big amount | Winning by one single vote | An election held in the mountains
test: In a video game, the hero wins an election by ripping down everyone else's posters. What is wrong? | Damaging others' posters is wrong; real campaigns persuade with speeches, leaflets and visits | Nothing, ripping posters is how real campaigns work | Posters do not exist in real elections | Only the king is allowed to rip posters
test: Mum, a bit dry, says, "Kindly note that my vote is my own business." What is she reminding you? | Voting is secret, and she does not have to tell anyone | She did not bother to vote at all this year, so it is private | Her vote does not count unless she tells someone who it was for | She will vote for whoever you choose for her next time
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the secrecy of the ballot is maintained. | The protection of the voter from intimidation and from inducement | The concealment of the total number of votes cast | The prevention of candidates from learning of their own candidacy | The acceleration of the counting process
exam: It is given that the margin between two candidates is exceedingly narrow. Which of the following most accurately characterises a subsequent recount? | A customary verification of the result, implying no impropriety | An admission of fraud on the part of the candidate requesting it | A procedure available exclusively to the declared winner | An unlawful attempt to reverse the will of the electorate
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning an exit poll. | An estimate derived from the responses of a sample of voters departing polling stations | The definitive result, declared upon the completion of the count | A record of all persons who have voted by post | A barrier erected at the exit of a polling station
exam: Determine the number of Electoral College votes requisite for election to the presidency of the United States. | Two hundred and seventy | Two hundred and sixty-nine | Five hundred and thirty-eight | Four hundred and thirty-five
